 3. 🤖 AI agent economy unfolds 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios. Stock: Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Axios CEO Jim VandeHei writes in his weekly C-Suite newsletter : 

 We're getting a live look into the post-app, post-web world: 

 Meta's new personal agent, Muse — the #1 free app on Apple's App Store in the U.S. — and Microsoft's new Copilot for Business are built on the same premise about where this is headed. Why it matters: We won't use apps (life) or software (work) to get things done. We'll tell AI agents, each running on its own cloud computer, and they'll do it for us. This is happening now.

 Ben Thompson, whose Stratechery is essential reading for tech thinkers, lays it out in his latest essay, " Apps, Agents, and Aggregation ." He can be dense and deep in the weeds. Read him anyway. 1. You (Muse): You text your agent, and it gets the job done. Once you grant permission, it plugs into your email, calendar, payments and shopping, doing the clicking and scheduling you'd otherwise do with apps and websites.

 That's why Amazon blocked Muse , Thompson explains: An agent shopping for you never sees or clicks on Amazon's ads. Instinct, an invite-only Muse competitor lighting up Silicon Valley, just raised $1B at a $10B valuation — 4x its value ONE MONTH earlier. 2. Your business (Copilot, OpenAI's dots, Muse for Small Business): Same idea, but built for the enterprise. The competition in this space is intense. Microsoft's new Autopilot works overnight, monitors Teams channels and handles follow-ups. You tag it like a colleague, and any employee can build and share an internal app or solution on the spot. Both Meta ( Muse for Small Business ) and OpenAI ( dots ) rolled out their versions this week. Anthropic's entire business is built around business.

 Thompson's bet: No human will touch clunky enterprise software again. Why would you, when an agent can customize what you need cheaply and instantly? 🥊 Reality check: Trust is the hurdle. Just 8% of Americans say they'd trust Meta with their passwords, and 58% wouldn't share them with any AI agent, according to an Oppenheimer survey .

