 6. 🦾 Behind the scenes: Racing to AGI 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Kevin Roose signs books yesterday at a breakfast for Tammy Haddad's Washington Women Technology Network/Washington AI Network. Photo: Mike Allen/Axios Kevin Roose — longtime New York Times tech writer and podcaster, now putting on his founder cap and launching the Machine Gods media company — is out with " The AGI Chronicles ," which he worked on for 12 years, about the race for artificial general intelligence, or humanlike AI capability.

 Roose, trying to capture what it's like to live in the Bay Area during the birth of humanity's most powerful technology, writes that in a reflective moment, Demis Hassabis — the Nobel laureate who chairs Google DeepMind and is Alphabet's chief scientist — "told a colleague that he wanted to build a control room in DeepMind's headquarters where he could watch AGI being born." Hassabis said he envisioned "a tiered spectator gallery, like the ones at NASA Mission Control, with huge arrays of screens on the wall and hundreds of stadium seats. He wanted everyone at DeepMind to sit there together on the day that AGI was released into the world, cheering for it as it cured diseases, made new scientific discoveries, and solved problems that had eluded humans for centuries. He wanted them to bear witness to the most important event in history — the birth of a new kind of god, a thinking machine capable of unlocking the mysteries of the universe." "Machine Gods," billing itself as "the official podcast of the singularity," launches the week of Oct. 19 with Roose and his former "Hard Fork" co-host, Platformer proprietor Casey Newton .

